<h2>Prerequisites</h2>
<ul>
    <li>You are <a href="../../login">logged in</a>.</li>
    <li>You have <a href="how-to-enable-creator-and-use-modules">activated Creator mode</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h1>Setting up custom commands</h1>
<p>Custom commands are a great way to add more functionality to your server. They allow you to create commands that can be used by your players. You can use them to create a custom help command, a command to teleport to a specific location, or even a command to give a player a specific item.</p>
<h2>Opening the custom command panel</h2>
<p>First, scroll down to the bottom of the Minecraft modules. You should find a module called <strong>Custom Commands</strong> with an <strong>edit</strong> button.</p>
<img src="../../i/g/using_custom_commands_with_the_minecraft_plugin/img0.png" alt="Where to find">
<p>Click on <strong>edit</strong> to edit your custom commands</p>
<h2>Configuring a command</h2>
<img class="float" src="../../i/g/using_custom_commands_with_the_minecraft_plugin/img1.png" alt="Configuration screen">
<p>Most of this is pretty easy & simple. Give your command a title, description and add a price. <br>Once you are done, scroll down to the bottom and click on <strong>Save</strong>.</p>
<h2>Viewer input</h2>
<p>You have probably noticed that there is a <strong>viewer input</strong> section for every command.<br> In the situation that you need viewer input, you can toggle this on. <br>Streamer Options will automatically replace <strong>%arg%</strong> (in your command) with the input.</p>
<p>There also is a placeholder input, this will guide the viewer what to input.</p>
<h2>Extra information</h2>
<p>You can also place <strong>%streamer%</strong> within your command, this will make the command loop for every streamer (without it, it will run once), replacing <strong>%streamer%</strong> with their name.</p>